{
 "id": "2025-bellini-green",
 "name": "Green Design in Living Rooms & Bedrooms (restorativeness rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Bellini, Miola, Sperduti, Caccaro, Pinton, Graffeo & Pazzaglia (2025), \"Green design in living and bedroom spaces: exploring environmental restorativeness and affective qualities of spaces\", Frontiers in Psychology 16: 1631417. Participants are randomly assigned to one of two between-subject greenery styles: plants built into furniture modules (non-climbing) or climbing plants on supporting structures. They answer gender, age and the Inclusion of Nature in Self picture item, then see six rendered rooms in random order: a living room and a bedroom, each without greenery, with potted plants and with integrated greenery. Each room stays on screen while they rate four perceived-restorativeness items (being away, fascination, coherence, compatibility) and seven adjectives (restful, beautiful, inviting, lively, chaotic, oppressive, monotonous) on 1–7 scales. Answers are keyed by image (group_room_greenery). Original result (304 adults via Prolific, Italy): rooms with greenery were rated more restorative (PRS total 18.1–18.2 vs 16.2 without plants) and more beautiful, inviting and lively; integrating plants into the furniture made rooms more fascinating and less monotonous, but also more chaotic and less coherent than potted plants. Images: the 12 renderings printed in Figures 1–2 of the paper (CC BY 4.0). Differences from the original study: (1) the paper used three living rooms and three bedrooms per condition (36 images) balanced across participants; only the one living room and one bedroom per condition printed in the figures are available, so every participant in a group sees the same six images; (2) only the fascination item is quoted in the paper; the being-away, coherence and compatibility items are written for this template following the PRS subscale definitions; (3) the paper does not state the response scale for the PRS items; 1–7 is used, consistent with the reported means; (4) the Big Five Inventory (BFI-10), the Preference for Nature Questionnaire and education, income and residence questions are omitted; (5) English wording instead of the Italian versions.",
